v1.6.5-pre (in progress)
- ratio/seq: the optimal parser (levels 11-15) now prices with what the
  seq coder charges instead of the frame codec's codeword bytes: -log2
  frequency of the ll/ml/offset symbols plus their extra bits, and the
  literals' order-0 entropy (the byte prices charged a literal 8 bits and
  mispriced offsets both ways). The statistics come from a cheap level-3
  pre-parse, freed before the main context is allocated; level 15 adds
  one refinement re-parse. The frame HC codec keeps its byte prices and is
  byte-identical.
  Silesia L15 26.52% -> 25.77% (-2.84%; zstd -19's lead 1.56 -> 0.81
  points), L11 26.76% -> 26.17%; AIT L15 39.54% -> 38.82%. Per file L15:
  dickens -6%, reymont -6%, nci -5%, webster -4.7%, C -6%, B -4%.
  Decode gets faster too (fewer sequences): -8..9% cycles.
  Encode: L11 about the same speed, L15 ~2x slower (the refinement pass).
  Tried and rejected: the same prices in the heuristic parsers of levels
  4-10 (better_price / lowest_price split point): +2..3% larger at L6/L9,
  neutral at L4. Those greedy/lazy parsers decide one sequence at a time,
  and the byte model's 8-bit literal overprices literals in a way that
  compensates for their short view; only the full DP gains from true
  costs.

v1.6.4-pre
- fixed: the fast LZ encoder (-0, and the light-LZ fallback inside seq
  frames) crashed on Windows x86 (32-bit). On 32-bit hosts its hash table
  held pointers whose empty (NULL) entries were rejected only by the
  distance check; lz6's 16 MB window let NULL pass when the heap sits
  below 16 MB, as it does on Windows x86, and the encoder read address 0.
  It now always uses the index table (as 64-bit hosts already did); 64-bit
  output is unchanged. Present since the window grew to 16 MB (v1.6.3-pre
  win32 is affected; Linux x86 was not, its heap is high).
- perf/seq v2: the ll/ml/of symbols move from three rANS streams to
  table ANS (zstd FSE-style: one table load + a bit read per symbol, no
  multiply), and their states share ONE backward bitstream with every
  extra bit (ll/ml extras; offsets as bucket + raw bits). Six sections
  (3 rANS streams, 25 per-bucket offset top-8 streams, low bits, extra
  bits) become three small table headers and one stream; the decoder
  reads a sequence with one refill.
  BREAKS THE SEQ BLOCK FORMAT (lab format, no compatibility shim; the
  LZ6S1 fixture is regenerated). The frame HC levels are untouched.
  Measured (one core, interleaved vs master, Silesia): decode L2
  ~376 -> ~459 MB/s, L15 ~393 -> ~485 MB/s (cycles/byte 9.95 -> 8.29,
  9.26 -> 7.65). Size: Silesia L2 +0.27%, L15 +0.50% (the dropped
  offset model; mozilla/nci/sao/x-ray carry it); AIT L2 -0.06%, L15
  -0.04% (fewer stream headers).
  Tried and rejected: two interleaved sequence bitstreams (+6.5% cycles:
  the decoder is instruction-bound at IPC ~2.5, not latency-bound).
- perf/seq: run-time BMI2 dispatch for the decoder body (x86-64, GCC/
  clang): variable shifts cost 1 uop instead of 3; Silesia cycles/byte
  L15 7.97 -> 7.65, L2 8.73 -> 8.32. Tried and rejected: the same for the
  Huffman literal decoder (no change) and single-window field extraction
  in the sequence reader (~1%, not worth duplicating the reader).
- fixed: plane_encode leaked the Huffman candidate buffer when the LZ
  candidate won (found by ASAN on AIT G at level 1).
- api: LZ6_compress_fast_window() and LZ6_compress_HC_window() cap every
  match distance to (1 << windowLog) - 1, windowLog 10..24. The block
  format is unchanged; a decoder that keeps only the last 2^windowLog
  bytes can decode the output (e.g. a ZPAQL post-processor that must
  reserve its whole window per thread). The existing entry points are
  byte-identical to before (verified on dickens/samba/x-ray, fast and HC
  1/9/15). Measured cost on dickens HC -15: 2^22 +1.2%, 2^20 +5.5%.
- perf/seq decode: literals decoded in bulk before the sequence loop
  (two-symbol Huffman lookups, 4 lookups per 64-bit window), branch-free
  2-byte rANS renorm on budgeted unchecked blocks, 4-byte {f,off} table
  entries (L1-resident), 64-bit bit readers, two-stage sequence pipeline
  with match-source prefetch 16 sequences ahead, 16B wild copies with an
  exact tail, byte-sized offset top-8 arrays, single-pass plane
  interleave. Format unchanged; old streams decode.
  Measured (mem-to-mem, one core, interleaved vs master): Silesia L2
  ~259 -> ~374 MB/s, L15 ~271 -> ~390 MB/s; AIT L2 312 -> 398, L15
  259 -> 339.
- encode: rANS table precision capped at 12 bits (was 14/16 for larger
  alphabets) so every decode table takes the cache-resident layout.
  Output changes, format does not: Silesia L15 +0.011%, L2 +0.015%;
  AIT L15 +0.031%, L2 +0.036%.
- hardening: byte-plane blocks whose size is not a multiple of the
  stride are rejected (the tail used to be reported as decoded without
  being written; the encoder never emits them).

v1.6.3-pre
- perf/seq: the per-sequence repcode (previously a fixed 2-bit field on
  every sequence) is now folded into the offset FSE alphabet, so the
  model codes the joint distribution of (offset bucket, repcode) instead
  of paying 2 bits per sequence regardless of use. The field's real
  entropy is ~0.6 bits on text (only 12% of sequences use a repcode) and
  ~1.6-2.0 on binary. One less bitfield read per sequence, so decode gets
  faster as well.
  BREAKS THE SEQ BLOCK FORMAT: .lz6 files written by older seq levels no
  longer decode (lab format, no compatibility shim). The frame HC levels
  (--hc) are untouched and stay byte-identical.
  Measured (single block, level 15): Silesia tar 62,437,010 -> 58,693,799
  (-6.0%), level 2 76,742,753 -> 73,509,428 (-4.2%), AIT 5,367,989 ->
  5,194,253 (-3.2%); x-ray -15.1%, webster -7.6%, dickens -8.4%. Decode
  on the tar goes 149.5 -> 158.9 MB/s (L15).
- bakeoff: compat_LZ6S1_C.lz6s1 regenerated with the current block format
  (bakeoff/mk_legacy_fixture.c rebuilds it); the decode-only LZ6S1
  envelope shim is unchanged and verified against it.

v1.6.2-pre
- fixed: heap-buffer-overflow in the content-segmented loop when a
  segment landed exactly on blockSize (-B1/-B2 crashed or corrupted
  the heap; found by CLI audit + ASAN)
- fixed: truncated output after a size-triggered flush (only one block
  written; -B3/-B4 decoded to partial data with exit 0)
- fixed: legacy LZ6S1 fixture used the pre-9a177e4 block format; the
  compat shim failed on it (Error 46) — fixture regenerated, shim verified
- verified: all block sizes -B1..-B7 round-trip with seq and HC levels
  (8-file AIT matrix, incompressible and 200MB+ inputs; the small-block
  dst bound is covered by LZ6F_compressFrameBound(blockSize))

v1.6.1-pre
- seq is now the compression engine of levels 1-15 inside the standard
  .lz6 frame (the --seq side mode is gone); new magic-less per-block
  codec dispatch (LZ6S2: seq / light-LZ / raw escape in one frame)
- LZ6S3: content-segmented blocks — the CLI cuts blocks at entropy
  profile shifts so each region gets its own literal mode and transform
- fixed: plane transform skipped the normal pass on text (the lane-skew
  gate passed on novels) — dickens 57.16% -> 32.95%, reymont to 24.20%
- fixed: light-LZ fallback clobbered the seq payload when it lost
- seq block default size B7 (single block up to 256MB); fixed blockwalk
  diagnostic; MinGW binary-mode (pipes) fix; LZ6_SEG_MINSEG/THRESHOLD knobs
- decode: streaming literals, dual-layout FSE tables, order-1 combined
  entries, 16B inlined copies — Silesia L15 ~120 -> ~200 MB/s
- encode: rep-stack MTF-3 in all HC strategies; 32MB windows; hash
  widening by density (chain strategies); 32-bit __uint128_t fallback
- Silesia.tar CLI: L2 42.81% -> 36.21%, L15 29.46% -> 28.34%; AIT
  A-H L2 5,597,278 -> 5,497,008, L15 unchanged

v1.6.0-pre
- Windows x64/x86 cross-compile (static, Win7 SP1+): build_windows.sh
- hardened decoder bounds (fuzz gate), frame format with 0x184D2207
  seq-coded frames, 256MB chunk streaming, lz6io/CLI I/O error reporting

v1.5
- introduced compatibility with Visual C++ 2010 and newer
- attached Visual Studio 2010 project
- thoroughly tested with 21 Travis CI and 7 AppVeyor CI tests
- fixed bug with reusing a context in lz6frame.c (LZ6F_compressBegin and LZ6_compress_HC_continue)
- fixed rare bug in match finder (concerns levels 4 - 15)

v1.4.1
- fixed bug with a backward match extension (level 11 and 12)

v1.4
